    "product_id": "10ff09e4-9854-4cb7-abe6-15478cbb8d30",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 131153\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n353 AM PST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Unsettled weather returns to the region today and lasts through \n  Saturday\n\n* Additional precipitation and cooler weather arrives Monday and \n  Tuesday and again in the second half of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through next Thursday\n\nThe pattern changes today as a trough and low pressure dip into \nthe region with strong southerly winds building into the mid to \nlate morning with precipitation close to follow the initial wave \nof stronger winds. Wind Advisories are in effect Friday into early \nSaturday with 20 to 30 mph sustained southerly winds and gusts to \n55 mph. The first areas affected will be the eastern Sierra by the\nlate morning. A Winter Storm Warning is in effect for elevations \nabove 7,000 feet in the Eastern Sierra for Friday into Saturday \nafternoon. Around a foot of new snow will be possible in the \nSierra Crest, and 5 to 10 inches of snow expected between 7,000 ft\nand 9,500 ft. Rain chances and winds spread across the region \ninto Friday afternoon. Rain shadowing and a poor moisture feed \nwill prevent much in the way of rainfall in the desert valleys, \nbut still offer some measurable amounts for higher elevations. The\nstrongest winds will favor high elevations, and will spread to \nthe lower valleys in the eastern portions of the CWA, but the \nwestern half wont see the hazardously windy conditions. Winds ease\nthrough Saturday and rain looks to exit that night, leading to \ncalmer conditions for Sunday.\n\nBack to unsettled weather for Monday as a low pressure builds into \nthe area early that morning and spreads through the day. The \nbetter feed of moisture for this system doesn't line up until \nafter the low pressure center crosses the Sierra, limiting Sierra \nsnow potential, but leading to better rainfall for the rest of the\nregion. Rain looks to become widespread into Monday afternoon for\nthe areas east of the Sierra Nevada, but rain rates look to stay \nlight to moderate. Rainfall totals from Monday into Tuesday look \nmodest (0.10 to 0.25\") for all but Northeast Clark Co, Mohave Co, \nand Lincoln Co which will range from 0.30 to 1.0\" after showers \nexit to the east. Wednesday will offer another pause in rain, but \nanother batch of showers arrives early Thursday and could linger \ninto next Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Winds\nwill be light through late morning, then shift to the southeast \nand increase to 10-12KT with intermittent gusts to around 20KT \nexpected through mid-afternoon. Winds then veer to the southwest, \nwith gusts persisting through mid-evening, and another round of \ngusts to around 25KT expected overnight as sustained speeds remain\nbetween 10-15KT. Ceilings will remain SCT-BKN around 10-15kft, \nand while showers are not expected in the Las Vegas Valley at this\ntime, terrain obscuration is expected with rain and snow showers \ndeveloping over surrounding mountains this afternoon and tonight. \n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Persistent\nsoutheasterly wind gusts to around 25KT in the northern Owens \nValley/KBIH will continue, increasing to 30-35KT this afternoon. \nOvernight, winds at KBIH will shift to the north, remaining gusty\nthrough the end of the forecast period. Elsewhere, light winds \nthis morning will shift to the south late morning into early \nafternoon, and then to the southwest, with gusts to 20-25KT common\nthrough tonight. Stronger gusts are expected across the \nsouthwestern Great Basin and across the western Mojave Desert/KDAG\nwhere gusts to 30-35KT are expected tonight. Clouds will be SCT- \nBKN around 10kft, possibly lower with any showers that develop. \nConfidence is too low to include SHRA mention at any terminals, \nthough KBIH could see VCSH late this evening. Terrain obscuration \nis expected with rain and snow showers over area mountains.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
